---
// Moved verbatim from terms.html. Astro bundles the imported CSS into the page's <head>.
import BrandSymbol from "../components/BrandSymbol.astro";
import ThemeProvider from "../components/ThemeProvider.astro";
import "../style.css";
import "../legal.css";

const site = Astro.site!.origin;
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <ThemeProvider />
    <title>Terms of Service · relayrunner</title>
    <meta
      name="description"
      content="The terms for using the hosted RelayRunner service, run by the maintainers of the open source project."
    />
    <meta name="theme-color" content="#ffffff" media="(prefers-color-scheme: light)" />
    <meta name="theme-color" content="#0b0d10" media="(prefers-color-scheme: dark)" />
    <link rel="canonical" href={`${site}/terms`} />
    <link rel="icon" href="/favicon.png" type="image/png" />
    <link rel="apple-touch-icon" href="/apple-touch-icon.png" />
  </head>
  <body>
    <a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a>

    <header class="site-header">
      <div class="wrap header-row">
        <a class="brand" href="/" aria-label="relayrunner home">
          <BrandSymbol />
          relayrunner
        </a>
        <nav aria-label="Primary">
          <ul class="nav-links">
            <li><a href="/privacy">Privacy</a></li>
            <li>
              <a class="btn-ghost btn-sm" href="https://github.com/relayrunner/relayrunner"
                >GitHub</a
              >
            </li>
          </ul>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </header>

    <main id="main" class="wrap legal">
      <h1>Terms of Service</h1>
      {/* For a change people should agree to again, also bump LEGAL_VERSIONS.terms (packages/activity/src/legal.ts). */}
      <p class="updated">Last updated November 9, 2026</p>

      <p class="intro">
        RelayRunner is an open source project run by its maintainers, not a company. These terms
        cover the hosted service they operate: the dashboard at app.relayrunner.app, its API and
        webhooks, the RelayRunner GitHub Apps, RelayRunner's hosted runners, and the
        <code>relayrunner</code> CLI when it talks to that service. "RelayRunner", "we" and "us"
        mean those maintainers. By using the service you agree to these terms and to our
        <a href="/privacy">Privacy Policy</a>. If you use it for an organization, you confirm you're
        allowed to accept these terms for it.
      </p>

      <h2>The code and the service</h2>
      <p>
        RelayRunner's source code is on
        <a href="https://github.com/relayrunner/relayrunner">GitHub</a> under the license in its
        repository. These terms don't limit what that license lets you do with the code. They only
        cover the hosted service. You're always free to run RelayRunner yourself instead.
      </p>

      <h2>Early access</h2>
      <p>
        The service is new and still changing. Features may change or go away, it may be unavailable
        at times, and we may shut it down. Keep a GitHub-hosted fallback in your relay jobs so CI
        keeps running when RelayRunner can't route a job. The service is free during early access.
        If we ever charge for it, we'll tell you before you're billed anything.
      </p>

      <h2>Your account</h2>
      <p>
        You need a GitHub account to sign in, and you're responsible for what happens under your
        account and on machines you connect. Keep machine tokens private. If you think your account
        or a machine has been compromised, disconnect it and email
        <a href="mailto:security@relayrunner.app">security@relayrunner.app</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Your code, workflows and machines</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          You keep all rights to your code, workflows and data. You let us process them only as
          needed to run the service, as described in the <a href="/privacy">Privacy Policy</a>.
        </li>
        <li>
          A self-hosted runner executes whatever a workflow tells it to, as your user, on your
          machine. You decide which repositories and jobs can run on your machines, and you're
          responsible for who can change those workflows. RelayRunner never routes public
          repositories' jobs to connected machines, but that isn't a substitute for your own care.
        </li>
        <li>You're responsible for having the rights to the code and workflows you run.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>GitHub and other providers</h2>
      <p>
        Your use of GitHub, Blacksmith and other runner providers is governed by their own terms.
        What they charge, including GitHub Actions minutes used when a job falls back to
        GitHub-hosted runners, is between you and them. The minutes and savings RelayRunner shows
        are estimates.
      </p>

      <h2>Acceptable use</h2>
      <p>Don't use the service to:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>break the law or infringe anyone's rights;</li>
        <li>
          mine cryptocurrency, attack or scan other systems, send spam, or host malware on hosted
          runners or lint checks;
        </li>
        <li>access workspaces, machines or data that aren't yours;</li>
        <li>
          disrupt or overload the service, or get around its limits. Good-faith security research is
          welcome: report what you find to
          <a href="mailto:security@relayrunner.app">security@relayrunner.app</a> and give us
          reasonable time to fix it before you share it.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Stopping</h2>
      <p>
        You can stop at any time: uninstall the GitHub Apps, disconnect your machines, and email
        <a href="mailto:privacy@relayrunner.app">privacy@relayrunner.app</a> to delete your account.
        We may suspend or end your access if you break these terms or to protect the service or
        other people. If we shut the service down, we'll give notice where we reasonably can.
      </p>

      <h2>No warranty</h2>
      <p class="caps">
        The service is provided "as is" and "as available", without warranties of any kind, express
        or implied, including warranties of merchantability, fitness for a particular purpose and
        non-infringement. We don't promise that it will be uninterrupted, error-free or secure, or
        that it will route any particular job.
      </p>

      <h2>Limitation of liability</h2>
      <p class="caps">
        To the fullest extent the law allows, RelayRunner and its maintainers and contributors won't
        be liable for any indirect, incidental, special, consequential or punitive damages, or for
        lost profits, data, or CI minutes and other charges from third parties, arising from your
        use of the service. Our total liability for any claim about the service is limited to the
        greater of what you paid us for it in the 12 months before the claim, or US$50.
      </p>

      <h2>Indemnity</h2>
      <p>
        If someone makes a claim against us because of your code, your workflows or your breach of
        these terms, you'll cover the reasonable costs of that claim.
      </p>

      <h2>Changes</h2>
      <p>
        If we change these terms, we'll post the new version here and update the date above. Using
        the service after that means you accept the new terms.
      </p>

      <h2>Contact</h2>
      <p>
        Questions about these terms or your data:
        <a href="mailto:privacy@relayrunner.app">privacy@relayrunner.app</a>. Security reports:
        <a href="mailto:security@relayrunner.app">security@relayrunner.app</a>. Anything else:
        <a href="https://github.com/relayrunner/relayrunner/issues">GitHub issues</a>.
      </p>
    </main>

    <footer class="site-footer">
      <div class="wrap footer-row">
        <a class="brand brand-sm" href="/">
          <BrandSymbol />
          relayrunner
        </a>
        <ul>
          <li><a href="/docs">Docs</a></li>
          <li><a href="https://github.com/relayrunner/relayrunner">GitHub</a></li>
          <li><a href="https://github.com/apps/relayrunner">GitHub App</a></li>
          <li><a href="/privacy">Privacy</a></li>
          <li><a href="/terms" aria-current="page">Terms</a></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </footer>
  </body>
</html>
